WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:04.539
I often thought about telling Joe that I'd stolen the food, but I never did.

2
00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:09.372
He called us "ever the best of friends." How could I disappoint him? One

3
00:00:09.373 --> 00:00:14.611
evening about a year later, we were sitting by the kitchen fire waiting for my

4
00:00:14.612 --> 00:00:17.943
sister to come home. She burst through the door with Joe's uncle, Mr. Pumblechook. "If

5
00:00:17.944 --> 00:00:26.118
this boy isn't grateful tonight, he'll never be," she said excitedly. "Miss Havisham wants

6
00:00:26.119 --> 00:00:29.284
Pip to go play at her house." "Miss Havisham who lives uptown?" asked Joe. "Is

7
00:00:29.285 --> 00:00:31.118
there any other?" I'd heard that Miss Havisham was an immensely rich and grim

8
00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:39.094
lady, who lived a secluded life in a large, dismal house barricaded against robbers. "Well,

9
00:00:39.095 --> 00:00:43.006
I wonder how she knows Pip," said Joe. "Who said she knew him?" answered my

10
00:00:43.007 --> 00:00:48.760
sister. "It's Uncle Pumblechook who made the arrangements. This boy's fortune may be made

11
00:00:48.761 --> 00:00:52.405
by going to Miss Havisham's!" Mr. Pumblechook beamed. "But here I am wasting time talking

12
00:00:52.406 --> 00:00:54.262
when Uncle Pumblechook is waiting and this boy's filthy from head to toe." My sister

13
00:00:54.263 --> 00:01:00.262
grabbed a tub of water and started scrubbing me. Then she dressed me in

14
00:01:00.263 --> 00:01:03.493
my tightest suit and sent me off with Mr. Pumblechook for the night. "God bless

15
00:01:03.494 --> 00:01:08.231
you, Pip!" cried Joe as we rode away. The next morning Mr. Pumblechook took me

16
00:01:08.232 --> 00:01:11.668
to Miss Havisham's old brick house. Some of the windows had been sealed up,

17
00:01:11.669 --> 00:01:16.144
and those on the lower floor were covered with bars. While we waited at the

18
00:01:16.145 --> 00:01:21.215
gate, I saw there was a large, abandoned brewery at the side of the

19
00:01:21.216 --> 00:01:26.731
house. A girl came across the courtyard and unlocked the gate. "This," said Mr. Pumblechook

20
00:01:26.732 --> 00:01:30.556
"is Pip." "Come in, Pip," said the girl, who was very pretty and seemed quite

21
00:01:30.557 --> 00:01:34.064
haughty. Mr. Pumblechook was also coming in when she stopped him with the gate.

22
00:01:34.065 --> 00:01:38.985
"Oh!" she said. "Did you wish to see Miss Havisham?" "If Miss Havisham wishes to

23
00:01:38.986 --> 00:01:42.794
see me," said Mr. Pumblechook. "Ah! But you see, she doesn't." She said it in

24
00:01:42.795 --> 00:01:47.199
such a final way that Mr. Pumblechook could not protest. But he eyed me

25
00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:54.112
severely as if I had done something to him. I looked at the abandoned brewery

26
00:01:54.113 --> 00:01:58.366
as we crossed the courtyard. "Don't loiter, boy," snapped the girl. Though she called

27
00:01:58.367 --> 00:02:02.033
me "boy," she was about my age. She was as scornful of me as if

28
00:02:02.034 --> 00:02:05.382
she were 21 and a queen. Since the great front entrance had two chains across

29
00:02:05.383 --> 00:02:11.763
it, we went into the house by a side door. The first thing I

30
00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:16.342
noticed was that the passages were all dark. The girl picked up a candle and

31
00:02:16.343 --> 00:02:21.755
led me upstairs to the door of a room. "Go in." "After you, miss." "Don't

32
00:02:21.756 --> 00:02:25.358
be ridiculous, boy. I'm not going in." She walked away, taking the candle with

33
00:02:25.359 --> 00:02:29.683
her. I was half afraid, but I knocked on the door and a voice told

34
00:02:29.684 --> 00:02:31.017
me to enter. I found myself in a lady's dressing room well-lit with candles. No

35
00:02:31.018 --> 00:02:32.675
glimpse of daylight was to be found. And in an armchair sat the strangest

36
00:02:32.676 --> 00:02:39.818
lady I have ever seen. She was dressed in rich white fabrics—satin, lace, and silk.

37
00:02:39.819 --> 00:02:44.159
In her hair she wore a wedding veil and flowers like a bride, but

38
00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.255
her hair was white. I saw that she hadn't quite finished dressing; her handkerchief, gloves,

39
00:02:48.256 --> 00:02:54.747
some flowers, and a prayer book all lay in a heap on her dressing table.

40
00:02:54.748 --> 00:02:59.889
I also began to realize that everything that should have been white was now

41
00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:05.897
faded and yellow, and the bride had withered like the flowers. "Come here," said Miss

42
00:03:05.898 --> 00:03:14.151
Havisham. "Let me look at you." It was when I stood before her, avoiding her

43
00:03:14.152 --> 00:03:21.635
eyes, that I noticed her watch had stopped at twenty minutes to nine. A

44
00:03:21.636 --> 00:03:26.231
clock in the room had also stopped at that time. "Look at me," she said.

45
00:03:26.232 --> 00:03:32.707
"You're not afraid of a woman who hasn't seen the sun for years?" "No," I

46
00:03:32.708 --> 00:03:35.786
lied. "Do you know what I touch here?" She laid her hands on her

47
00:03:35.787 --> 00:03:42.032
left side. "Your heart?" "Broken!" She uttered the word with a weird smile that had

48
00:03:42.033 --> 00:03:44.524
a kind of boast in it. "I sometimes have strange fancies, and I have

49
00:03:44.525 --> 00:03:49.279
the strange fancy to see someone play, so play, play, play!" Nothing could have been

50
00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:50.603
harder for me at that moment. We stared at each other until she said, "What's

51
00:03:50.604 --> 00:03:56.095
wrong? Are you stubborn?" "No, ma'am. I'm very sorry for you and sorry I

52
00:03:56.096 --> 00:04:05.516
can't play now. If you complain I know I'll get in trouble with my sister.

53
00:04:05.517 --> 00:04:08.254
But it's so strange here, so melancholy . . ." "So strange to him," she

54
00:04:08.255 --> 00:04:14.024
muttered. "So familiar to me. So melancholy to us both. Call Estella." I left

55
00:04:14.025 --> 00:04:18.833
the room to call the girl. She answered at last, and her light came along

56
00:04:18.834 --> 00:04:25.246
the passage like a star. Miss Havisham beckoned Estella to come near. She picked up

57
00:04:25.247 --> 00:04:34.579
a jewel from the table and held it against the girl's pretty brown hair.

58
00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:41.317
"Yours one day, my dear, and you will use it well. Let me see you

59
00:04:41.318 --> 00:04:48.563
play cards with this boy." "With this boy? Why, he's a common laboring boy!"

60
00:04:48.564 --> 00:04:55.555
I thought I overheard Miss Havisham's answer, but it seemed so strange: "Well, you can

61
00:04:55.556 --> 00:05:00.063
break his heart." So we sat down to play cards, and I began to understand

62
00:05:00.064 --> 00:05:08.222
that everything in the room, like the watch and the clock, had stopped a

63
00:05:08.223 --> 00:05:16.389
long time ago. Miss Havisham sat as still as a corpse while we played. "What

64
00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:19.714
rough hands he has!" said Estella before our first game was over. "And what thick

65
00:05:19.715 --> 00:05:26.690
boots!" When I misdealt the cards in our next game, she called me a

66
00:05:26.691 --> 00:05:32.611
stupid, clumsy laboring boy. "She says many bad things about you," said Miss Havisham, "but

67
00:05:32.612 --> 00:05:40.841
you say nothing about her. What do you think of her?" I didn't want

68
00:05:40.842 --> 00:05:51.341
to say it out loud, so I whispered in Miss Havisham's ear, "I think she's

69
00:05:51.342 --> 00:05:54.675
very haughty and very pretty and very insulting. I'd like to go home." "And never

70
00:05:54.676 --> 00:05:58.341
see her again though she is so pretty?" "I'm not sure about that, but

71
00:05:58.342 --> 00:06:04.666
I'd like to go home now." Miss Havisham made us finish the game and told

72
00:06:04.667 --> 00:06:10.587
me to come back in a week. Then she told Estella to feed me. While

73
00:06:10.588 --> 00:06:13.817
I waited for her in the courtyard, I took the opportunity to look at

74
00:06:13.818 --> 00:06:19.396
my rough hands and thick boots. They had never troubled me before, but now they

75
00:06:19.397 --> 00:06:23.396
did. Estella returned with some food, which she handed to me as if I were

76
00:06:23.397 --> 00:06:26.896
a dog in disgrace. I was so humiliated and angry that tears came to

77
00:06:26.897 --> 00:06:31.650
my eyes. Estella looked at me with delight at almost making me cry. This gave

78
00:06:31.651 --> 00:06:37.888
me the power to control myself, but as soon as she left, I wept.

79
00:06:37.889 --> 00:06:43.467
When I recovered, I explored the abandoned brewery until I saw Estella coming to let

80
00:06:43.468 --> 00:06:45.380
me out of the courtyard. She opened the gate and stood holding it. "Why don't

81
00:06:45.381 --> 00:06:49.126
you cry?" she asked. "Because I don't want to." "Yes, you do. You're almost

82
00:06:49.127 --> 00:06:53.913
crying now." She laughed cruelly, pushed me out the gate, and shut it behind me.

